# ---------- 1. PADEL RACKET FOR TENNIS PLAYERS ----------
tp = f'''<header class="hero"><div class="wrap"><h1>Best padel racket for tennis players, and the tennis habits to drop</h1>
<p class="lead">Tennis players pick up padel fast, but their first racket is often wrong: too heavy, too head-heavy or a diamond. Tell us about your tennis and your arm. You get the padel specs to look for and the three tennis habits that will cost you the most points.</p>
{quick("Most tennis players should start with a teardrop or round padel racket of about 350 to 365 g, with a low to medium balance and a soft or medium core. Skip diamonds and rackets of 370 g or more until you have played about a season and your bandeja is reliable. With tennis elbow, go lighter (about 345 to 355 g), low balance, soft core and a fibreglass face.", "2026-11-28")}</div></header>
<section style="padding-top:10px"><div class="wrap"><div class="quiz">
<div class="card">
{_bq2("tp", "tl", "Your tennis level", [("rec", "Social or club"), ("comp", "League or competitive"), ("ex", "High level or ex-college")], "rec")}
{_bq2("tp", "st", "Your tennis game", [("base", "Baseliner, topspin"), ("srv", "Big serve, flat hitting"), ("net", "All-court, I like the net")], "base")}
{_bq2("tp", "arm", "Tennis elbow or arm pain?", [("no", "No"), ("some", "Now and then"), ("yes", "Yes, often")], "no")}
{_bq2("tp", "pd", "How much padel have you played?", [("0", "None yet"), ("few", "A few sessions"), ("season", "A season or more")], "0")}
</div>
<aside class="result" id="tp-out" aria-live="polite"><span class="pill b">Your padel racket</span><h2 id="tp-h" style="margin-top:10px">350 to 360 g, teardrop</h2><p id="tp-p"></p><dl class="spec" id="tp-spec"></dl>
<h3 style="margin-top:14px;font-size:1rem">Tennis habits to drop first</h3><ol id="tp-hab" class="tp-hab"></ol><p id="tp-t" class="wr-src"></p>
<div class="btns" style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn lime sm" type="button" id="tp-copy">Copy result</button></div></aside>
</div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">General buying guidance with no brand claims. The weight and shape bands come from where three 2025 to 2026 padel guides for tennis players agree (sources at the bottom); the picker's rules are our own.</p></div></section>
{AD.format("tp-mid")}
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>Padel racket vs tennis racket: what is different?</h2></div>
<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Padel racket</th><th>Tennis racket</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Hitting surface</td><td>Solid face with holes, over a foam (EVA) core. No strings.</td><td>Strings in an open frame</td></tr>
<tr><td>Length</td><td>Short: 45.5 cm at most under the rules</td><td>Much longer, usually about 27 inches (69 cm)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Where the power comes from</td><td>The core and face. Short, compact swings.</td><td>String bed and a long swing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weight</td><td>Most are about 340 to 375 g</td><td>Varies widely</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wrist cord</td><td>Yes. Wear it: the court is small and your partner is close.</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grip</td><td>Continental for almost every shot</td><td>Eastern or semi-western for most forehands</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">The holes make the racket lighter and easier to move through the air. Because there are no strings, the core does the work a string bed does in tennis: a soft core holds the ball a little longer, which helps placement. More on materials: <a href="{href("padel-racket-materials.html")}">carbon vs fibreglass and EVA vs foam</a>.</p></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>Why tennis players should not buy a diamond racket first</h2></div>
<p>A diamond racket has its sweet spot high up and most of its weight in the head. That rewards a long, fast swing, which is exactly the tennis habit you need to lose. The guides we checked agree: start with a teardrop or round racket, and move to a diamond only when your overheads are steady. Padel Browser puts it at about a season of weekly play, once your bandejas land deep and you stop sending balls long off the back glass. Not sure which you are? Take the <a href="{href("control-vs-power-padel-racket.html")}">control vs power racket test</a>.</p></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>Which tennis habits do not work in padel?</h2></div><div class="grid">{_cards([
    ("The long backswing", "The ball comes fast and close, often off a wall. A short, compact swing gives you time and control. Every guide we read puts this first."),
    ("Heavy topspin", "Topspin kicks the ball up off the back glass and gives the net players an easy volley. Hit flatter or with a little slice."),
    ("Hitting hard to win", "In padel the walls bring hard balls back. Points are won by placement, lobs and patience, not by power."),
    ("Staying on the baseline", "The team at the net usually wins the point. Get to the net with your partner after the serve and after a good lob."),
    ("Hitting before the glass", "Tennis players rush in to take the ball early. Let it come off the back wall, then play it: it slows down and you get more time."),
    ("Too much wrist", "A whippy wrist sends the ball long and loads the elbow. Keep the wrist firm and turn the shoulders."),
], pill="Habit")}</div>
<p style="margin-top:12px">What does transfer: your eye for the ball, footwork, volleys, the split step and your reading of the game. That is why tennis players often reach a decent level fast. To fix specific problems, use the <a href="{href("fix-my-padel.html")}">fix my padel tool</a>; to learn the wall, read <a href="{href("padel-back-glass.html")}">playing off the back glass</a>.</p></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>What padel racket is best for tennis elbow?</h2></div>
<p>If tennis gave you elbow trouble, choose comfort over power: about 345 to 355 g, a low (head-light) balance, a round or soft teardrop shape, a soft core and a fibreglass face, which flexes more than carbon. Avoid stiff, head-heavy diamonds, which can undo the benefit of a shorter racket. Pain has many causes, so get lasting pain checked by a doctor or physio. Check your own load with the <a href="{href("padel-elbow.html")}">padel elbow check</a>.</p></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>What next?</h2></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Exact weight</h3><p>Grams for your level, strength and arm: the <a href="{href("padel-racket-weight.html")}">racket weight calculator</a>.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>The serve</h3><p>Padel's serve is underarm, below the waist, after a bounce: <a href="{href("serve-rules.html")}">serve rules checker</a>.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Padel vs tennis</h3><p>Court, scoring and the walls side by side: <a href="{href("padel-vs-tennis-vs-pickleball.html")}">padel vs tennis vs pickleball</a>.</p></div>
</div></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="aff-box"><h3>Shop rackets for tennis players</h3><p>Check weight, balance and core on each product page against your specs above.</p><ul class="aff-links"><li><a href="{go("padel-teardrop-rackets")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Teardrop rackets</a></li><li><a href="{go("padel-round-rackets")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Round rackets</a></li><li><a href="{go("padel-black-friday-rackets")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Black Friday racket deals</a></li><li><a href="{go("padel-overgrips")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Overgrips</a></li><li><a href="{go("padel-shoes")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Padel shoes</a></li></ul>{AFF_NOTE}</div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:.9rem">Sources, checked 28 Nov 2026: Padel Browser "Best padel rackets for tennis players 2026" (27 May 2026: about 360 to 365 g, medium balance, teardrop first, soft EVA, skip 370 g+ for a season, diamond once bandejas land deep), Padel USA "Which padel racket should you buy if you come from tennis?" (26 Apr 2025: 350 to 365 g, low or medium balance, round or teardrop, soft or medium core, fibreglass for arm comfort, 45.5 cm max length, use the wrist strap), The Padel School "Padel racket vs tennis racket" (about 360 to 365 g, round or teardrop, avoid very stiff or head-heavy rackets, holes reduce weight and air resistance, tennis habits of over-swinging and hitting early). Written in our own words.</p></div></section>'''
tp_faq = [
    ("What padel racket should a tennis player buy?", "A teardrop or round racket of about 350 to 365 g with a low to medium balance and a soft or medium core. Leave diamonds and 370 g+ rackets until you have played about a season."),
    ("Can I use a tennis racket for padel?", "No. Padel needs a solid padel racket with no strings, 45.5 cm long at most. A tennis racket is too long for the small court and walls, and is not allowed in matches."),
    ("Is padel easy for tennis players?", "Tennis players usually learn fast because of their timing, footwork and volleys. The hard parts are the walls, the shorter swing and giving up topspin and power."),
    ("Should a tennis player use a heavy padel racket?", "Not at first. Strong tennis arms can swing a heavy racket, but padel is fast and close. About 355 to 365 g is plenty; heavier rackets pull you back into long tennis swings."),
    ("What grip do you use in padel if you play tennis?", "The continental grip, the same one you use for a tennis volley or serve, for almost every shot. Do not use a western forehand grip."),
    ("Is padel good for tennis elbow?", "It can be easier on the arm because the racket is short and the swings are compact, but a stiff, heavy, head-heavy racket can undo that. Go light, head-light and soft, and see a doctor or physio for lasting pain."),
]
page("padel-racket-for-tennis-players.html", "Best Padel Racket for Tennis Players: Specs + Picker",
     "Best padel racket for tennis players: weight, balance, shape and core by tennis level, tennis elbow advice, and the tennis habits that cost padel points.",
     tp, tp_faq, "Racket for tennis players")

# ---------- 2. PADEL RACKET FOR SPIN ----------
sn = f'''<header class="hero"><div class="wrap"><h1>Padel racket for spin: what really makes the ball spin?</h1>
<p class="lead">Rough faces, sand finishes and 3D textures are sold as spin. A lab test says the racket matters less than you think. Answer six questions and see where your spin is coming from, what is holding it back and whether a spin racket is worth buying.</p>
{quick("Spin in padel comes mostly from your swing: brushing across the ball with a slicing, high-to-low path. A rough face helps a little on glancing, brushing contacts, but a 2022 Tennis Warehouse University lab test found most rackets give about the same spin on most shots. If you want a spin racket, pick the right shape and weight first, then choose a textured (sand or 3D) carbon face, keep it clean and play with fresh balls.", "2026-11-28")}</div></header>
<section style="padding-top:10px"><div class="wrap"><div class="quiz">
<div class="card">
{_bq2("sn", "tech", "On bandejas and slices, do you brush across the ball?", [("0", "No, I hit it flat"), ("1", "Sometimes"), ("2", "Yes, a clear slice")], "1")}
{_bq2("sn", "face", "Your racket face", [("0", "Smooth"), ("1", "Painted grit"), ("2", "Sand or 3D texture")], "0")}
{_bq2("sn", "wear", "How worn is the face?", [("2", "New or nearly new"), ("1", "A few months"), ("0", "Worn smooth or shiny")], "2")}
{_bq2("sn", "ball", "Your balls", [("2", "New can"), ("1", "A few sessions old"), ("0", "Old and soft")], "1")}
{_bq2("sn", "clean", "Do you wipe the face during play?", [("0", "Never"), ("1", "Sometimes"), ("2", "Every few games")], "0")}
{_bq2("sn", "shape", "Your racket shape", [("0", "Round"), ("1", "Teardrop"), ("2", "Diamond")], "1")}
</div>
<aside class="result" id="sn-out" aria-live="polite"><span class="pill b">Your spin</span><h2 id="sn-h" style="margin-top:10px">Some spin</h2>
<div class="cp-scale" aria-hidden="true"><i id="sn-dot"></i></div><div class="cp-lab"><span>Little</span><span>Some</span><span>Lots</span></div>
<p id="sn-p"></p><h3 style="margin-top:14px;font-size:1rem">Biggest gains, in order</h3><ol id="sn-fix" class="tp-hab"></ol><dl class="spec" id="sn-spec"></dl><p id="sn-t" class="wr-src"></p>
<div class="btns" style="margin-top:10px"><button class="btn lime sm" type="button" id="sn-copy">Copy result</button></div></aside>
</div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">The checker weights technique most, because the lab test below found the swing, not the face, makes most of the difference. The scoring is our own.</p></div></section>
{AD.format("sn-mid")}
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>Does a rough padel racket give more spin?</h2></div>
<p>A little, and only on some shots. In 2022 Tennis Warehouse University fired balls at clamped padel rackets at many angles and filmed the spin. When the ball hit the face at a steep angle (most rackets above about 45 degrees, almost all above 60), it gripped the face and every racket produced about the same spin, rough or smooth. Only on shallow, glancing contacts, the kind you make when you brush hard across the ball, did the rougher faces hold the ball better while smoother ones let it slide.</p>
<div class="tbl" style="margin-top:12px"><table><thead><tr><th>Surface in the test</th><th>Grip (friction) score</th><th>Starts to slide below about</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Smoothest racket tested</td><td>0.18</td><td>53 degrees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Middle racket</td><td>0.33</td><td>40 degrees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roughest racket tested</td><td>0.50</td><td>28 degrees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sandpaper (for comparison)</td><td>8.2</td><td>Under 10 degrees</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">Angles are measured from the face: 90 degrees is a straight-on hit, low numbers are glancing, brushing contacts. Only three rackets were tested, so treat this as a guide, not a ranking. The takeaway: your swing path makes the spin; a textured face helps keep it on brushing shots like the víbora and slice.</p></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>What face textures are there?</h2></div>
<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Finish</th><th>What it is</th><th>Lasts</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Smooth</td><td>Plain gloss or matt face</td><td>Does not change</td></tr>
<tr><td>Painted grit</td><td>A rough coat sprayed on the face</td><td>Can wear slick quickly; Padel Browser warns within about a month of heavy play</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sand or silica finish</td><td>Grains bonded into the face</td><td>Longer than paint, but wears where you hit most</td></tr>
<tr><td>3D relief</td><td>Raised texture moulded into the face</td><td>Lasts much longer than a coat</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">Every texture loses some bite as it wears, and dust and sand on the face reduce grip too. A quick wipe with a dry cloth between games helps. Care tips: <a href="{href("racket-care.html")}">padel racket care</a>.</p></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>How do you put more spin on the ball in padel?</h2></div><div class="grid">{_cards([
    ("Swing high to low", "For a slice or bandeja, swing from above the ball and across it, with an open face. The path makes the spin."),
    ("Brush, do not punch", "Contact the back and side of the ball and keep the face moving across it, like you are cutting it."),
    ("Head speed", "Spin needs racket speed at contact. A relaxed grip and turning your shoulders give more speed than a tight wrist."),
    ("Fresh balls", "Balls lose pressure and felt as they age, and old balls grip less. Check yours with the dead ball checker."),
], pill="Tip")}</div>
<p style="margin-top:12px">Shot guides: <a href="{href("bandeja-vs-vibora.html")}">bandeja vs víbora</a>, <a href="{href("padel-smash.html")}">the smash</a> and <a href="{href("padel-lob.html")}">the lob</a>. Ball check: <a href="{href("padel-balls.html")}">dead ball checker</a>.</p></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sh"><h2>Is a spin racket worth it?</h2></div>
<p>Buy for shape, weight and balance first; spin is a bonus. A beginner with a flat swing gains almost nothing from a rough face. An improving player who already slices the bandeja and plays the víbora will notice a textured carbon face more, especially when it is new. Diamond rackets give more head speed for attacking players, but have a smaller, higher sweet spot. Find your type with the <a href="{href("control-vs-power-padel-racket.html")}">control vs power test</a> and the <a href="{href("racket-finder.html")}">racket finder</a>.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="aff-box"><h3>Shop rackets with spin in mind</h3><p>Look for "rough", "sand" or "3D" face finishes in the product details, after you have checked shape, weight and balance.</p><ul class="aff-links"><li><a href="{go("padel-teardrop-rackets")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Teardrop rackets</a></li><li><a href="{go("padel-diamond-rackets")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Diamond rackets</a></li><li><a href="{go("padel-black-friday-rackets")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Black Friday racket deals</a></li><li><a href="{go("padel-balls")}" {AFF_ATTR}>Fresh balls</a></li></ul>{AFF_NOTE}</div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:.9rem">Sources, checked 28 Nov 2026: Tennis Warehouse University "The physics of padel spin production" (Crawford Lindsey, 20 Nov 2022: lab method, friction scores, grip and slide angles, "most rackets produce about the same spin") and its padel spin comparison tool; Padel Browser "Best padel rackets for spin in 2026" (5 Jun 2026: sand, silica and 3D textures, painted coats can go slick quickly, moulded texture lasts longer, diamond gives more head speed, a dusty face and old balls lose bite). Written in our own words; we name no models.</p></div></section>'''
sn_faq = [
    ("Which padel racket gives the most spin?", "A textured (sand or 3D) carbon face gives a little more grip on brushing shots, but a lab test found most rackets give about the same spin on most shots. Your swing makes most of the spin."),
    ("Does a rough padel racket give more spin?", "Only a little, and mainly on glancing, brushing contacts. On straight-on hits the ball grips any face, so spin is about the same."),
    ("Does a rough padel face wear off?", "Yes. Painted grit can go smooth quickly with heavy use; sand finishes last longer and moulded 3D textures longest."),
    ("Are rough padel rackets legal?", "Textured faces are sold by all the big brands and used on the pro tour. If you play official competition, check your federation's racket rules."),
    ("How do I get more spin in padel?", "Swing high to low and across the ball with an open face, keep a relaxed grip for head speed, play with fresh balls and keep the face clean."),
    ("Should a beginner buy a spin racket?", "It is not worth paying extra. Choose a forgiving round or teardrop racket with the right weight; add spin with technique later."),
]
page("padel-racket-for-spin.html", "Padel Racket for Spin: Does a Rough Face Help? + Checker",
     "Padel racket for spin: what a lab test found about rough faces, sand and 3D textures, how long they last, how to add spin, and a free spin checker.",
     sn, sn_faq, "Racket for spin")

DEMAND_PAGES += ["padel-racket-for-tennis-players.html", "padel-racket-for-spin.html"]
